#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265016 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3996c79f-9281-5b9d-9b33-8863f10adc33" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "493cb774-b25e-50f8-b41f-b3c76836bff3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{1}{3},\\,t=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $t=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265018} {:answer/id #uuid "31d02854-adc8-5e41-87f7-91468b371dc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{5}{12},\\,t=\\frac{1}{3}$ and $t=\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265019} {:answer/id #uuid "3e562226-050c-5015-a114-3fe3afa8ce6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{1}{12},\\,t=-\\frac{1}{3}$ and $t=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265020} {:answer/id #uuid "e8b09f9a-ddef-583e-8d6e-ff84f09e10d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{5}{12},\\,t=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $t=-\\frac{1}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265021} {:answer/id #uuid "c069a518-0e37-56dc-91d3-1ee95e64df16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{1}{3},\\,t=-1$ and $t=\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31d02854-adc8-5e41-87f7-91468b371dc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{5}{12},\\,t=\\frac{1}{3}$ and $t=\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265019} :answer-field/id #uuid "0968de50-163c-5c9d-b148-50de0c694b64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265017}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "688e38cc-4ccf-5ec7-be78-915098c07c29" :question/math-academy-id "q-112605" :question/problem "The equation $-6{t}^{2}+5t-1=0$ can be written in the form $6(t-p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-6{t}^{2}+5t-1) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 6{t}^{2}-5t+1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we move the constant term to the right-hand side and complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{t}^{2}-5t+1 & =0 \\\\ 6{t}^{2}-5t & =-1 \\\\ 6({t}^{2}-\\frac{5}{6}t) & =-1 \\\\ 6[{t}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{5}{12}t+{(\\frac{5}{12})}^{2}-{(\\frac{5}{12})}^{2}] & =-1 \\\\ 6[{(t-\\frac{5}{12})}^{2}-\\frac{25}{144}] & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{(t-\\frac{5}{12})}^{2}-6\\cdot \\frac{25}{144} & =-1 \\\\ 6{(t-\\frac{5}{12})}^{2}-\\frac{25}{24} & =-1 \\\\ 6{(t-\\frac{5}{12})}^{2} & =-1+\\frac{25}{24} \\\\ 6{(t-\\frac{5}{12})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{24}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{5}{12}.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{(t-\\frac{5}{12})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{24} \\\\ {(t-\\frac{5}{12})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{144} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(t-\\frac{5}{12})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{144}} \\\\ |t-\\frac{5}{12}| & =\\frac{1}{12} \\\\ t-\\frac{5}{12} & =\\pm \\frac{1}{12} \\\\ t & =\\frac{5}{12}\\pm \\frac{1}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\frac{5}{12}-\\frac{1}{12}=\\frac{4}{12}=\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ t & =\\frac{5}{12}+\\frac{1}{12}=\\frac{6}{12}=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $t=\\frac{1}{3}$ and $t=\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265023 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3996c79f-9281-5b9d-9b33-8863f10adc33" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4123ddf6-626b-5a44-941d-f210606cad5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=2,\\,z=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and $z=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265025} {:answer/id #uuid "4bc38603-af41-5943-9e0a-6fb6cd35f86a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=2,\\,z=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $z=-\\frac{5}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265026} {:answer/id #uuid "d214f6eb-0cf1-5474-a5d1-13d543079fbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=9,\\,z=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $z=\\frac{3}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265027} {:answer/id #uuid "e15b678e-7f40-5ce5-a4aa-ce8d23a61f16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=9,\\,z=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $z=\\frac{9}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265028} {:answer/id #uuid "5feba206-b336-5449-85cb-b068936a9410" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=2,\\,z=-\\frac{7}{2}$ and $z=-\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265029}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5feba206-b336-5449-85cb-b068936a9410" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=2,\\,z=-\\frac{7}{2}$ and $z=-\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265029} :answer-field/id #uuid "249ccaa4-bc1f-51f4-bcf7-aac6975f42bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265024}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b0945056-0521-55d9-80fa-5db83ff53407" :question/math-academy-id "q-112612" :question/problem "The equation $-4{z}^{2}-16z-7=0$ can be written in the form $4(z+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-4{z}^{2}-16z-7) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 4{z}^{2}+16z+7 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we move the constant term to the right-hand side and complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{z}^{2}+16z+7 & =0 \\\\ 4{z}^{2}+16z & =-7 \\\\ 4({z}^{2}+4z) & =-7 \\\\ 4[{z}^{2}+2\\cdot 2\\cdot z+{(2)}^{2}-{(2)}^{2}] & =-7 \\\\ 4[{(z+2)}^{2}-4] & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(z+2)}^{2}-4\\cdot 4 & =-7 \\\\ 4{(z+2)}^{2}-16 & =-7 \\\\ 4{(z+2)}^{2} & =-7+16 \\\\ 4{(z+2)}^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=2.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(z+2)}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {(z+2)}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(z+2)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}} \\\\ |z+2| & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ z+2 & =\\pm \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ z & =-2\\pm \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =-2-\\frac{3}{2}=-\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ z & =-2+\\frac{3}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $z=-\\frac{7}{2}$ and $z=-\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265030 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "257b907c-70c1-5bbd-a6d2-297d51325d1f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5327ab0-394a-5838-ad3d-0e853e88d150" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=6,\\,$ $z=-1,\\,5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265032} {:answer/id #uuid "becb00e6-4435-5a4e-9421-799f3d729713" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=9,\\,$ $z=-1,\\,5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265033} {:answer/id #uuid "c475a3a7-3124-5e00-adcf-f126228398be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=5,\\,$ $z=2,\\,3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265034} {:answer/id #uuid "d23d3a1a-7abc-52fe-91bd-d46cb953f947" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=9,\\,$ $z=2,\\,3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265035} {:answer/id #uuid "069b71b1-0358-5fe7-9697-48312bef4c9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=5,\\,$ $z=-1,\\,5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265036}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "becb00e6-4435-5a4e-9421-799f3d729713" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=9,\\,$ $z=-1,\\,5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265033} :answer-field/id #uuid "c277acd0-20e5-538d-8a15-e62b73befdc1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265031}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2779e37c-82bb-5252-8304-35569aa4b2f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-141627" :question/problem "The equation $3{z}^{2}-15=12z$ can be written in the form $(z-p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, divide it by the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{z}^{2}-15 & =12z \\\\ 3{z}^{2}-12z & =15 \\\\ 3({z}^{2}-4z) & =15 \\\\ {z}^{2}-4z & =5 \\\\ {z}^{2}-2\\cdot 2\\cdot z & =5 \\\\ {z}^{2}-2\\cdot 2\\cdot z+{2}^{2}-{2}^{2} & =5 \\\\ {(z-2)}^{2}-{2}^{2} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(z-2)}^{2}-{2}^{2} & =5 \\\\ {(z-2)}^{2}-4 & =5 \\\\ {(z-2)}^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=2$ and $q=9.$\n\nNext, we solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{(z-2)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\ |z-2| & =3 \\\\ z-2 & =\\pm 3 \\\\ z & =2\\pm 3 \\\\ z & =-1,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265037 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "257b907c-70c1-5bbd-a6d2-297d51325d1f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3c0352c-1868-5c1c-b425-3853fb4e8ffd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-6,\\,z=-1\\pm \\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265039} {:answer/id #uuid "6878f65e-f2fd-5544-a060-ca16811e6d2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-1,\\,z=-1\\pm \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265040} {:answer/id #uuid "0cce48ca-62a3-5be4-bb58-e70ec1cc15cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=1,\\,z=1\\pm \\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265041} {:answer/id #uuid "c5a08141-29d3-510c-a194-682f6ba3056e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=1,\\,z=-1\\pm \\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265042} {:answer/id #uuid "1cf0b4dd-ab43-5a5d-8dd8-59374ec6dbbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=1,\\,z=1\\pm \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265043}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5a08141-29d3-510c-a194-682f6ba3056e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=1,\\,z=-1\\pm \\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265042} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ffa9472-acd2-55a9-883f-f178097bdc75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265038}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "74bd8e3a-a67b-574b-924d-9b4365f760ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-3431" :question/problem "The equation $2{z}^{2}+4z=10$  can be written in the form $(z+p)^{2}+q=0.$ Find $p,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we divide out by the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{z}^{2}+4z & =10 \\\\ 2({z}^{2}+2z) & =10 \\\\ {z}^{2}+2z & =5 \\\\ {z}^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot z & =5 \\\\ {z}^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot z+{1}^{2}-{1}^{2} & =5 \\\\ {(z+1)}^{2}-{1}^{2} & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(z+1)}^{2}-{1}^{2} & =5 \\\\ {(z+1)}^{2}-1 & =5 \\\\ {(z+1)}^{2}-6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, $p=1$ and $q=-6.$ \n\nNow, let's solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(z+1)^{2}-6 & =0 \\\\ (z+1)^{2} & =6 \\\\ \\sqrt{(z+1)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{6} \\\\ |z+1| & =\\sqrt{6} \\\\ z+1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{6} \\\\ z & =-1\\pm \\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265044 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3996c79f-9281-5b9d-9b33-8863f10adc33" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fca80544-35ba-5e55-b254-adfa666685c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=25,\\,w=\\frac{5}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{15}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265046} {:answer/id #uuid "a905c2a5-b340-595b-b17e-ff29d9123205" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=15,\\,w=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{15}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265047} {:answer/id #uuid "faec4bb9-d593-53c5-a78f-61bcc1c988e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=25,\\,w=5$ and $w=\\frac{11}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265048} {:answer/id #uuid "700c3a74-8d88-5f57-aa8f-3d89ac539faa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=15,\\,w=\\frac{5}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{15}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265049} {:answer/id #uuid "e8c9b527-9629-5cee-85ac-2d7533bce7c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=25,\\,w=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}$ and $w=15$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265050}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fca80544-35ba-5e55-b254-adfa666685c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=25,\\,w=\\frac{5}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{15}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265046} :answer-field/id #uuid "88ad550e-5aa5-582a-8acc-cd4b26d7afdf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265045}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "211d779e-92bf-5786-beb2-56b9bec0ebf9" :question/math-academy-id "q-86415" :question/problem "The equation $-4{w}^{2}+40w-75=0$ can be written in the form $4(w+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-4{w}^{2}+40w-75) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 4{w}^{2}-40w+75 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{w}^{2}-40w+75 & =0 \\\\ 4{w}^{2}-40w & =-75 \\\\ 4({w}^{2}-10w) & =-75 \\\\ 4[{w}^{2}-2\\cdot 5w+{5}^{2}-{5}^{2}] & =-75 \\\\ 4[{(w-5)}^{2}-25] & =-75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(w-5)}^{2}-4\\cdot 25 & =-75 \\\\ 4{(w-5)}^{2}-100 & =-75 \\\\ 4{(w-5)}^{2} & =-75+100 \\\\ 4{(w-5)}^{2} & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $q=25.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(w-5)}^{2} & =25 \\\\ {(w-5)}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(w-5)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{25}{4}} \\\\ |w-5| & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ w-5 & =\\pm \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ w & =5\\pm \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w & =5+\\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{15}{2} \\\\ w & =5-\\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $w=\\frac{5}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{15}{2}.$"}]}
